<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Society of Mary (Marists)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1836</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Marists, Marist Fathers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Society of Mary, whose members are known as Marists, is a Catholic religious congregation founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> by Jean-Claude Colin and approved by Pope Gregory XVI in 1836. The same year it was entrusted with missions in the western Pacific. Marist priests converted Wallis and Futuna, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-chanel">Pierre Chanel</a> was killed in 1841, and worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, Samoa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Islands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/society-of-mary-marists.jpg" alt="Society of Mary (Marists)" width="180" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Society of Mary · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marist_Crest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
